  "content": {
    "id": "p37-q14-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "14",
    "prompt": "Maverick and Loshi are playing darts. For any one shot, the probability that Maverick hits the bullseye is $\\frac{1}{4}$. The probability that Loshi hits the bullseye is $\\frac{1}{2}$. If each player has three shots, find the probability that:",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p37-q14-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "they hit the bullseye the same number of times",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{17}{64}$.\n\nSum the probabilities of both players scoring 0, 1, 2 or 3 bullseyes.",
          "worked": "Let $M$ and $L$ be Maverick's and Loshi's numbers of bullseyes. Their shots are independent, with $M\\sim\\operatorname{Bin}(3,\\frac14)$ and $L\\sim\\operatorname{Bin}(3,\\frac12)$.\n\nFor $k=0,1,2,3$,\n$$P(M=k)=\\binom{3}{k}\\left(\\frac14\\right)^k\\left(\\frac34\\right)^{3-k},\\qquad P(L=k)=\\binom{3}{k}\\left(\\frac12\\right)^3.$$\nThe matching outcomes are $(0,0),(1,1),(2,2),(3,3)$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(M=L)&=\\sum_{k=0}^{3}P(M=k)P(L=k)\\\\P(M=L)&=\\frac{27}{64}\\times\\frac18+\\frac{27}{64}\\times\\frac38+\\frac9{64}\\times\\frac38+\\frac1{64}\\times\\frac18\\\\P(M=L)&=\\frac{27+81+27+1}{512}\\\\P(M=L)&=\\frac{17}{64}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 55
      },
      {
        "id": "p37-q14-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "they hit the bullseye a combined total of two times.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{171}{512}$.\n\nSum the probabilities of the pairs $(0,2)$, $(1,1)$ and $(2,0)$.",
          "worked": "Let $M$ and $L$ be Maverick's and Loshi's numbers of bullseyes. Use the binomial probabilities with $n=3$, and success probabilities $\\frac14$ and $\\frac12$, respectively. The possible pairs are $(0,2),(1,1),(2,0)$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(M+L=2)&=P(M=0)P(L=2)+P(M=1)P(L=1)+P(M=2)P(L=0)\\\\P(M+L=2)&=\\frac{27}{64}\\times\\frac38+\\frac{27}{64}\\times\\frac38+\\frac9{64}\\times\\frac18\\\\P(M+L=2)&=\\frac{81+81+9}{512}\\\\P(M+L=2)&=\\frac{171}{512}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 42
      }
    ]
  },
